            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



**The main floor rear patio door, was leaky especially around the bottom right corner. Ensure that weather stripping creates a tight seal around the entire frame of the door and replace it if necessary. 



**The basement and utility room showed air leakage in many locations. Around the steel beams along exterior walls, around ducting, and along the sub-floor connection with the walls were the largest gaps to be sealed up. Any of these issues could be fixed by using a spray foam application in the open spaces. Both exterior doors in the basement were also leaky all around the outside edge and weather stripping should be replaced here. Since this area is part of your heated space, connected via the open basement ceiling/ first floor it is important to ensure this room is air tight.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 100 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 4.1 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main walls of your home appear to be 2x6 construction and batt insulation, with uninsulated strapping on the inside (R20). I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior (total R35), to bring your walls up to the current code for new buildings in Yellowknife. Due to the large amount of labour involved in adding insulation to the exterior of your home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 470 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.6 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 2200 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

BASEMENT WALLS



The basement walls appear to be 2x4 construction with batt insulation (R10). I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation (total R25) to the exterior. This insulation upgrade should be done in conjunction with a siding and insulation upgrade to the main walls.



Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement walls, you could potentially save $ 110 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.3 GJ, and qualify for a rebate of up to $1110.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</foundation>
                <windows>WINDOWS



All of the windows in this house (20 total between the basement and main floor) are vinyl frame, double pane, low E, argon filled windows, that are in good condition. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 200 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 8.0 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



For the savings you would see from a window upgrade, and considering the cost, the best time to complete a window upgrade would be during a siding and insulation retrofit.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</windows>
                <HeatingSystem />
                <AdditionalComments>ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



** I modelled your house for the situation of using your 2nd floor fireplace to account for 50% of the heating requirements needed for that floor, using wood delivery costs of approximately $400 a cord, and the result was that savings were minimal. This is because your boiler is highly efficient, and uses propane as a fuel source, making for cheap heating of your home already.



** I also modelled using a pellet stove to provide 50% of the required heat for the1st floor. For this addition there were savings, but not enough to warrent the installation cost of a pellet stove.



** Cold Master Bedroom - As was discussed on site, it's believed that the location of the thermostat that serves the master bedroom is such that heat from the adjacent zone (the two bedrooms across the hall), is 'tricking' that thermostat to think the master bedroom is warm. To fix this problem, the first thing we suggest trying is installing a programmable, smart thermostat with the capability to install a sensor in the master bedroom. Having a sensor in the bedroom will allow the thermostat to average the temperature from in the room, and in the hall, which should result in more heat being delivered to that space. The downside is that any other room on the same zone as the master bedroom may become overheated. If the programmable thermostat option does not solve your problem you will need to consult with a HVAC contractor to investigate the option of changing the zone arrangement.



Programmable thermostats qualify for a rebate of 50% of the purchase cost, up to a maximum of $100.



** LED lighting: Any light bulbs that burn out going forward should be replaced with LED bulbs, as energy reduction of greater than 50% per bulb can be seen when compared to traditional incandescent bulbs or fluorescent tubes.



Rebates are available from AEA for LED light bulbs up to 50% of purchase cost or a maximum of $200 in hydro communities, like Yellowknife.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



The water circulation pump in your home is a non-ECM pump and could be upgraded to an ECM pump to acheive immediate cost savings, with a low capital cost.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 minimum, $0.33 per kWh

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor, Scott Dowler



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Scott Dowler, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: scott.dowler@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
